<Base title="Projects Workbench" noindex={true}>
  <div class="wrap">
    {!configured && (
      <p class="note">
        This deployment has no database configured. Everything published stays
        available; only the working surface is unavailable.
      </p>
    )}

    {signin === 'cancelled' && (
      <p class="note" role="status">
        Sign in was cancelled, or the sign-in link expired. Nothing happened.
        Start again when you are ready.
      </p>
    )}
    {signin === 'password' && (
      <p class="note" role="alert">That email and password did not match.</p>
    )}
    {signin === 'oauth' && <p class="note" role="alert">Sign in could not start. Try again.</p>}
    {signin === 'exchange' && <p class="note" role="alert">Sign in did not complete. Try again.</p>}
    {signin === 'no_code' && <p class="note" role="alert">Sign in did not complete. Try again.</p>}
    {signin === 'domain' && (
      <p class="note" role="alert">
        Only a {org.name} account can sign in here
        {(org.verifiedDomains ?? []).length ? ` (${(org.verifiedDomains ?? []).join(' or ')})` : ''}.
        Nothing was saved. Sign in with your school account.
      </p>
    )}
    {signin === 'stale' && (
      <p class="note" role="alert">Your earlier session is no longer valid here. Sign in again.</p>
    )}
    {signin === 'closed' && (
      <p class="note" role="alert">
        SciPath is by invitation at {org.name} for now: there is no account for that address, and none is made by signing in. Nothing was saved. If you are in the class, ask your teacher to check the roster.
      </p>
    )}

    {/* **Why the address changed.**
    
        An account belongs to one school, so signing in at another school's
        address sends somebody home. That redirect used to be silent: you
        typed one hostname, arrived at a different one, and nothing said why.
        Somebody who does not notice keeps the wrong bookmark; somebody who
        does notice assumes it went wrong.
    
        The parameter is a slug and is never rendered. `org.name` is read from
        the tenant this page already resolved, so what appears is the school's
        own name — a query string cannot put text on the screen. */}
    {switchedHome && (
      <p class="note flash" role="status">
        Signed in at {org.name}. Your account is here, so that is where this
        address takes you.
      </p>
    )}

    {configured && !session && (
      <section class="hero">
        <p class="t-lbl">{org.name}</p>
        <h1 class="t-big">Sign in to work on a project</h1>
        {/* **A school account is right for a school and wrong here.**
        
            This said "Use your school Google account" on every tenant,
            including the open one, where there is no school and the first
            real signup was a personal Gmail address. A sentence that tells
            somebody the thing they just did is not allowed is worse than no
            sentence. */}
        <p class="lede">
          {org.signupMode === 'open'
            ? 'Sign in with Google. Any Google account works, including a personal one.'
            : 'Use your school Google account.'}
        </p>
        <div class="row" style="margin-top:24px">
          <a
            class="btn"
            href={next === HOME ? '/auth/signin/' : `/auth/signin/?next=${encodeURIComponent(next)}`}
          >Sign in with Google</a>
        </div>
        {/* The way round Google's own stumble (2.9). A district's
            session-length policy signs everybody out of Google every so
            often, and Google's password step on the way back into a
            third-party app has been seen to answer "Something went wrong ·
            Restart" and nothing else, until the person signs in to Google
            itself. Opening the school's mail does that; the link below opens
            it in a new tab so this page is still here to come back to. */}
        {org.signupMode === 'domain' && org.verifiedDomains.length > 0 && (
          <details class="signin-help">
            <summary>Google said "Something went wrong"?</summary>
            <p class="t-sm">
              That is Google asking you to sign in to your school account again, and its sign-in box sometimes fails on the way into another app.
              <a href={`https://mail.google.com/a/${org.verifiedDomains[0]}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open your school Gmail in a new tab</a>, sign in there, then come back here and press <b>Sign in with Google</b> again.
            </p>
          </details>
        )}

        {/* **Kept on every tenant, and the gate was wrong.**
        
            This was briefly hidden behind `org.demo`, on the reasoning that a
            form nothing the public possesses can satisfy is a dead end rather
            than a second way in. The reasoning holds and the gate did not:
            the platform tenant is not a demonstration tenant, so the flag
            removed sign-in from the one address every fixture account is
            actually used at, and the demonstration this exists for could not
            be given.
        
            A gate that hides a control from the person who needs it is worse
            than a control the public will not use. If this is ever hidden
            again it has to be on something that is true in development and
            false in production, which `demo: true` is not — it marks one
            tenant, not one environment. */}
        <div class="fixture">
          <p class="t-lbl">Or with your email and password</p>
